--TEST--
A property read, a method call or a property write through a union of classes is `E0881` when one class lacks the member, two classes give it different types or signatures, the member is not visible, or one class does not allow the write — `rule:types/a-member-every-class-of-a-union-declares`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Box {
    public string $name = "boxed";
    public string $title = "box";
    public function constructor() { }
    public function label(): string { return "box"; }
    public function size(int $scale): int { return $scale; }
    public function secret(): string { return "box"; }
}
final class Circle {
    public int $size = 1;
    public function constructor() { }
}
final class Label { public ?string $name = null; public function constructor() { } }
final class Tag {
    public readonly string $title;
    public function constructor() { $this->title = "tag"; }
    public function label(): string { return "tag"; }
    public function size(string $scale): int { return 1; }
    private function secret(): string { return "tag"; }
}

// `Circle` has no `$name`, so the read does not compile.
Box|Circle $either = new Box();
echo $either->name, "\n";

// Both classes have `$name`, but the types differ, so the read does not compile.
// The `??` after it reports nothing more.
Box|Label $titled = new Box();
echo $titled->name ?? "none", "\n";

// `Circle` has no method `label`, so the call does not compile.
echo $either->label(), "\n";

// `Box::size` takes an `int` and `Tag::size` takes a `string`, so the call does not compile.
Box|Tag $tagged = new Box();
echo $tagged->size(2), "\n";

// `Tag::secret` is `private`, so the call does not compile here.
echo $tagged->secret(), "\n";

// `Tag::$title` is `readonly`, so the write does not compile.
$tagged->title = "new";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0881]: `Circle` has no property `$name`
   --> %s:25:6
   |
25 | echo $either->name, "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used through `Box|Circle` here
   = help: you can use a member through a union only when every class has it with the same type, and it is visible here. Otherwise, test the value with `is` first

error[E0881]: `Box::$name` is `string`, and `Label::$name` is `string|null`, so the property has no one type
   --> %s:30:6
   |
30 | echo $titled->name ?? "none", "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used through `Box|Label` here
   = help: you can use a member through a union only when every class has it with the same type, and it is visible here. Otherwise, test the value with `is` first

error[E0881]: `Circle` has no method `label()`
   --> %s:33:6
   |
33 | echo $either->label(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used through `Box|Circle` here
   = help: you can use a member through a union only when every class has it with the same type, and it is visible here. Otherwise, test the value with `is` first

error[E0881]: `Box::size()` and `Tag::size()` do not have the same parameters and return type
   --> %s:37:6
   |
37 | echo $tagged->size(2), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used through `Box|Tag` here
   = help: you can use a member through a union only when every class has it with the same type, and it is visible here. Otherwise, test the value with `is` first

error[E0881]: `Tag::secret()` is private, so it is not visible here
   --> %s:40:6
   |
40 | echo $tagged->secret(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used through `Box|Tag` here
   = help: you can use a member through a union only when every class has it with the same type, and it is visible here. Otherwise, test the value with `is` first

error[E0881]: `Tag::$title` is `readonly`, so it cannot be written here
   --> %s:43:1
   |
43 | $tagged->title = "new";
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used through `Box|Tag` here
   = help: you can use a member through a union only when every class has it with the same type, and it is visible here. Otherwise, test the value with `is` first

